 <section id="firms">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="num">04</span><h2>Advisory-firm view — signal vs marketing</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">The firms sell the transformation they describe (₹50L–10cr+ engagements in India). Read direction as signal, magnitude as marketing. Their consensus, then the correction:</p>
  <div class="consensus"><ul>
   <li><b>2026 = agentic inflection year;</b> BFSI leads adoption; most institutions are in “pilot purgatory.”</li>
   <li><b>The bottleneck is the operating model,</b> not the model — and the regulated core is automated last.</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div class="firm-grid"><article class="firm"><h3>McKinsey</h3><p>Most-likely scenario = moderate adoption → ~15–20% cost cuts; full replacement rated &lt;5% likely. 50–60% of bank FTEs sit in operations — the prime target. Credit-memo agent case: 20–60% productivity, ~30% faster TAT.</p></article>
<article class="firm"><h3>BCG</h3><p>Boldest numbers: +30% profitability, −30–40% costs by 2030. Only ~25% of banks have AI in strategy; 75% in pilots. Warns agentic AI cuts fraud-running cost ~90%.</p></article>
<article class="firm"><h3>KPMG</h3><p>Best on India regulation — wrote the widely-cited FREE-AI explainer. ~$50bn agentic spend 2025; agent users report ~55% efficiency, ~35% cost cuts. Governance-first.</p></article>
<article class="firm"><h3>EY</h3><p>Most India-specific: ~42% of Indian FIs investing in AI/GenAI, ~74% running PoCs scaling into ops and underwriting. NBFCs called the fastest adopters.</p></article>
<article class="firm"><h3>Deloitte</h3><p>Most measured on timing: ~50% of GenAI adopters will pilot agentic by 2027. Highest near-term impact: customer support.</p></article>
<article class="firm"><h3>PwC</h3><p>Productivity headlines: up to 90% time savings in targeted processes; ~80% cycle-time cuts in matching. Pushing AI-enabled audit in India.</p></article></div>
  <div class="callout">
   <h3>RBI FREE-AI (13 Aug 2025) — the binding constraint</h3>
   <p><strong>Accountability:</strong> the lender is responsible for an AI decision regardless of autonomy. <strong>Understandable by Design:</strong> systems must be explainable by the deployer. Sector readiness, per RBI’s own survey:</p>
   <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><b>~21%</b><span>of entities deploy AI at all</span></div><div class="stat"><b>15%</b><span>use interpretability tools</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>18%</b><span>maintain audit logs</span></div><div class="stat"><b>21%</b><span>monitor model drift</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>⅓</b><span>have board-level AI oversight</span></div>
   </div>
  </div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
   <thead><tr><th>Consulting claim</th><th>The Indian-lending qualifier</th></tr></thead>
   <tbody><tr><td class="dv-claim">“Agentic AI transforms the lending lifecycle.”</td><td class="dv-real">True for pipeline, servicing, collections, monitoring. The <strong>credit decision stays human-governed</strong> — FREE-AI Accountability makes it a legal requirement.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="dv-claim">“30–40% cost cuts / 90% time savings.”</td><td class="dv-real">Global best-case, mostly back-office. The grounded Indian number: L&T Helios ~30% TAT cut on a <strong>co-pilot</strong>.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="dv-claim">“75% stuck in pilots — move now.”</td><td class="dv-real">India’s base is lower: ~21% deploy AI at all; responsible-AI readiness in low double digits. Constraint is <strong>foundations</strong>.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="dv-claim">“Autonomous agents are here.”</td><td class="dv-real">The one autonomous Indian lender (Trust Paisa) is <strong>small-ticket digital-native</strong>. Nobody runs it on secured or MSME books.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="dv-claim">“This is a technology decision.”</td><td class="dv-real">It is an <strong>operating-model and governance</strong> decision — and in India, a <strong>regulatory</strong> decision first.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 </section>

